the question that we really wanted to  answer with this project was you know  the same types of vaccines and places  the western developed economies which  are highly vaccinated are we seeing a  different impact in these places or is  the effect of the vaccines really the  same and so what we did was to take the  places that had vaccination coverage of  over 55  um and bigger places for populations  over 1 million and then we further  narrowed this to a group of places that  have seen a wave of cases pre and  post-vaccination in some places  vaccinations seem to be able to bring  deaths down to a much bigger level and  the question really was why and what can  the world learn from that  so of course there are many factors that  affect fatalities the analysis is really  only looking at the impact of  vaccination there's so many other things  that do come into play of course but  some of the big takeaways that we could  see in that contrast between places that  really managed to get dust really down  after vaccination was the length of the  interval between the first and second  vaccine dose did seem to play an  important role so for the european  countries that managed to bring  fatalities down to just 10 of what they  were at the previous peak you know we  saw uh in places like denmark and the uk  and germany for example they were  administering vaccine doses six weeks  apart in the uk that was up to eight to  twelve weeks apart whereas um in places  that have brought thefts down to a  smaller level the us japan israel they  were administering vaccine doses only  three to four weeks apart we've seen  that those longer intervals actually do  boost the immunity level much higher  we've seen this now from studies the  second dose is more powerful when the  first dose has been given that time to  fully mature in the body and that could  play an important role and there's a lot  of lessons there for countries that are  now still ongoing their vaccination  campaign and so what happened to those  earliest vaccinating countries by the  time delta came through and started  spreading across the world those initial  doses we do see the immunity has already  weighed and that could have played a  part in the fact that vaccination did  not provide as strong of a shield  towards delta compared to other  countries  so i mean the global vaccination  campaign is still ongoing there are so  many countries especially in the  developing world that haven't that don't  have any meaningful vaccination  protection at all and with the virus  becoming endemic we do know that  vaccinations may become a regular thing  what this analysis can tell us you know  is to give scientists policymakers an  inkling of some of the things that can  be done to strengthen that shield of  immunity against new variants because as  we know there is a great fear that delta  is not the only new mutation that's  going to come the world could be served  by another new variant and with the  vaccination strategy things like longer  dosage intervals waning immunity that  could influence how countries now  continue with their vaccination progress  and how they can get that shield of  immunity even stronger  [Music]
 

